


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: shanahan
@title: Charles Seeger's Tin Can, Bertrand Bronson's Star, and the Consequences of Indigestion
@presenter: shanahan
@abstract: 

The tools underlying computational musicology, corpus
studies, and music information retrieval each focus on
questions that have been debated for nearly a century. For
example, what does it mean to take the continuous stream of
music and discretize it into something to be read by a
machine? Or, when performance and notation are in such
misalignment, what is a machine-driven analysis actually
able to tell us? Charles Seeger embarked upon the question
to transcribe musical performance without the interference
of transcription through his melograph, and Bertrand
Bronson sought to encode entire musical traditions onto IBM
punch cards, but each repeatedly ran into questions about
how the musical object was being distorted by their
instruments of choice. This talk approaches these early
devices as epistemic things — objects that generated the
very research questions they were built to answer. Drawing
on recent archival research, it reconstructs what Seeger
and Bronson believed their machines could do and, just as
importantly, what they acknowledged their machines could
not. Here I argue that, while many of the compromises made
in these early studies persist in the field today, they are
too often positioned as technological constraints rather
than the broader epistemological impasses that they were
initially understood to be.


@@END: PRESENTATION



